---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const idx = projects.findIndex((p) => p.slug === "atlassian");
const project = projects[idx];
const next = projects[(idx + 1) % projects.length];
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL; // "/portfolio-presence/full-time/"

const tldr = [
  { k: "Space", v: "Enterprise ITSM, mobile" },
  { k: "Product", v: "Mobile on-call scheduling, inside a company-level platform consolidation" },
  { k: "Method", v: "Task-based remote moderated usability (90 min) with SEQ, on a prototype" },
  { k: "Sample", v: "11 analysed of 12 recruited — 6 on-call engineers, 5 engineering & ops managers" },
  { k: "Partnered with", v: "Product, design, and engineering (triad)" },
  { k: "Outcome", v: "Mobile on-call priorities reframed; 4 design changes shipped, 2 roadmap additions" },
];
---
<Layout
  title="On-call scheduling research — Anmol Bhargava"
  description="Evaluative research on mobile on-call scheduling in enterprise ITSM that shifted the team's question from making scheduling faster to investing in the parts of the on-call workflow mobile actually serves."
>
  <article class="section section--cream">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 820px;">
      <a href={`${base}#work`} class="back-link">← Selected work</a>
      <div class="case-meta">Project {project.num} · {project.meta}</div>
      <h1 class="display display--xl" style="color: var(--brick);">{project.title}</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 1rem;">{project.subtitle}</p>
      <div class="case-tags">
        {project.tags.map((t) => <span class="tag">{t}</span>)}
      </div>

      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <div class="tldr">
        <div class="tldr__label">TL;DR</div>
        <div class="tldr__grid">
          {tldr.map((item) => (
            <div>
              <div class="tldr__k">{item.k}</div>
              <div class="tldr__v">{item.v}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="tldr__shift">
          <div class="tldr__k">Strategic shift</div>
          <div class="tldr__v">
            “How do we make scheduling faster on mobile?” → “Which parts of the on-call workflow
            does mobile actually carry?”
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Problem -->
      <div class="section-label">The problem</div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><span>A 2am page has to land cleanly: right person, right shift, no ambiguity. In ITSM, ambiguity is operational risk</span></li>
        <li><span>The company was consolidating its incident-management product into the flagship platform — an OKR-level bet, mobile included</span></li>
        <li><span>Team's logic: faster, easier scheduling on mobile → adoption</span></li>
        <li><span>The assumption underneath: scheduling is what mobile on-call users need most</span></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- The question underneath -->
      <div class="section-label">The question underneath</div>
      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">Why not just validate?</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Prior research: users open the mobile app to acknowledge and close alerts — not to
          browse schedules.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">The evaluative question</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Can engineers and managers operate the new scheduling components?
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">The strategic question</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Is scheduling actually where mobile earns its place in the on-call workflow?
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- How it was run -->
      <div class="section-label">How it was run</div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><span>Tactical method for decisions in flight: 90-min moderated sessions, task-based, SEQ after each task</span></li>
        <li><span>Two cohorts = the workflow's two actors: engineers live the schedule, managers are accountable for it</span></li>
        <li><span>Stakeholder interviews with design and PM before writing the discussion guide</span></li>
        <li><span>Screener and prototype piloted with internal on-call engineers</span></li>
        <li><span>Sessions observed live by the triad, tagged in Dovetail as they happened</span></li>
        <li><span>1 of 12 excluded on a screener mismatch — 11 analysed</span></li>
        <li><span>Journey mapping declared out of scope up front</span></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Findings -->
      <div class="section-label">What we found</div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">Engineers kept drifting toward alerts</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          4 of 6 engineers checked their day via the alerts tab and called it the app's most
          useful part. Schedule admin is occasional; alerts are daily.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">The workflow runs on predictability, not speed</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          The one-tap “take a shift for an hour” met shrugs — schedules are set well in advance.
          Users wanted weeks visible, not seconds saved.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">Learned patterns beat novel ones</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Colour worked as memory for people and shifts. Familiar override paths succeeded; the
          novel one confused.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">One screen, two different jobs</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Managers never take call — they wanted whole-team visibility: coverage, gaps, who can
          fill a slot.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verbatim">
          <p>
            “As a manager, I am never gonna fill in for anybody's absence. I am not gonna sit and
            take incident calls.”
          </p>
          <div class="verbatim__attr">— P7, engineering manager</div>
        </blockquote>
      </div>

      <!-- The key insight -->
      <div class="kx">
        <div class="kx__kicker">The key insight</div>
        <div class="kx__eq">
          <span class="kx__term">Predictability + learnability</span>
          <span class="kx__gt">&gt;</span>
          <span class="kx__term kx__term--dim">simply reducing task time</span>
        </div>
        <p class="kx__body">
          Mobile's value: knowing what's coming and trusting learned patterns — not finishing
          tasks faster. A converging qualitative read, not a measured effect.
        </p>
        <ul class="kx__ev">
          <li>Schedules set well in advance — the one-tap speed feature met shrugs</li>
          <li>Users asked to see weeks ahead, here and in prior studies</li>
          <li>Colour used as memory for people and shifts</li>
          <li>Familiar override paths succeeded; the novel one confused</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- What changed -->
      <div class="section-label">What changed</div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><span>Team question reframed: invest where mobile carries the workflow — overrides, alert awareness, team visibility</span></li>
        <li><span>Manager view: recommended rebuild around team visibility, not personal schedules</span></li>
        <li><span>Card-view customisation deprioritised — weakest task, and participants said so themselves</span></li>
        <li><span>4 design changes shipped; 2 features added to the roadmap</span></li>
        <li><span>+15% satisfaction, ~+10% adoption post-launch (the team's measures, not this study's claim)</span></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Stakeholders -->
      <div class="section-label">How I worked with the team</div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><span>Triad throughout — researcher, designer, PM, with engineering close</span></li>
        <li><span>Insights soft-shared and sparred on before the readout — no big reveal</span></li>
        <li><span>Delivered as verbatims, Loom clips, and how-might-we prompts design could act on</span></li>
        <li><span>Decisions stayed with product; research kept them anchored to the real workflow</span></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Why it matters -->
      <div class="section-label">Why this matters in enterprise</div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><span>The unit of analysis is the workflow, not the screen — value is decided across actors, hand-offs, and tool ecosystems</span></li>
        <li><span>Feature requests are signals, not specs: users asked for colour customisation; the need was continuity of learned patterns</span></li>
        <li><span>The costliest failure mode isn't a missed usability issue — it's a roadmap confidently optimising the wrong problem</span></li>
        <li><span>Research pays off at the decision layer: what gets built, what gets deprioritised, which assumption the roadmap drops</span></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Takeaway -->
      <div class="pullquote">
        <div class="pullquote__label">Takeaway</div>
        <p class="pullquote__text">
          Speed is easy to measure, which makes it easy to over-value. This workflow ran on
          predictability — and research's job was noticing which problem deserved the screen.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href={`${base}projects/${next.slug}`} class="btn btn--filled-brick">
          Next: {next.title.length > 40 ? next.title.slice(0, 40) + "…" : next.title} →
        </a>
        <a href={`${base}#contact`} class="btn">Get in touch</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .verbatim {
    margin: 1rem 0 0.25rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--cream-soft);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--brick);
    border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  .verbatim p {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .verbatim__attr {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .kx {
    background: var(--deep);
    color: var(--on-brick);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: 2.5rem 2.25rem;
    margin: 3.5rem 0;
  }
  .kx__kicker {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    opacity: 0.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .kx__eq {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.6rem 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .kx__term {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  .kx__term--dim {
    opacity: 0.55;
  }
  .kx__gt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.2rem);
    color: var(--peach);
  }
  .kx__body {
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    opacity: 0.92;
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .kx__ev {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .kx__ev {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    }
  }
  .kx__ev li {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    position: relative;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
  .kx__ev li::before {
    content: "→";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--peach);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
